{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target claims reliability is a 'phantom of variance reduction' and reduces the comparison to mean accuracy only, but the problem asks to identify evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. The target's stress-test mechanism (introducing a known error not on the checklist) addresses this, but it defines 'genuine reliability' circularly: if the panel misses the off-checklist error, the target declares the gain 'merely' compliance-byproduct, yet if the panel catches it, the same logic would deny the gain is from the checklist at all. The target never produces the requested discriminating evidence because its test conflates 'scope restriction' with 'non-genuine reliability' without justifying why scope-restricted reliability is not genuine for tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes — where being reliably right on the tested scope IS the reliability the problem concerns.","counterexample":[{"task":"verifiable quality inspection of manufactured parts","panel":"follows checklist covering 10 known defect types, catches 98% of defects in those categories across 500 items","expert":"catches 96% overall but varies wildly (70%-100%) across sessions due to fatigue/intuition","off_checklist_defect":"novel surface micro-cracking not on checklist","result":"panel misses novel defect, expert catches some; yet panel's restricted-scope reliability is still higher on the verifiable outcomes the task actually scores"}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}